WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.790
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I am going to move the Board
on now to item five, which is the update on

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:18.050
the implementation of the recommendations
of the Cancer Taskforce. Cally Palmer will

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:24.430
join us. Cally is our National Cancer Director,
and also the Director of the Royal Marsden

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:29.700
Hospital in London. I think what we might
do, Bruce, is I might invite you to speak

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.880
first to the paper, and then Cally to follow.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:38.800
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: Thank you, Chairman. I was
not going to say very much. We have five very

00:00:38.809 --> 00:00:47.560
significant corporate priorities for the Board:
mental health, learning disabilities, the

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:54.550
reorganisation of urgent and emergency care,
diabetes and, of course, cancer. The Chief

00:00:54.550 --> 00:01:01.260
Executive asked Harpal Kumar, the Chief Executive
of Cancer Research UK, to undertake a review

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:07.530
of what we might be able to do to improve
cancer services in this country, and he set

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:14.399
out in his report a series of proposals which
the cancer community at large, I think, sign

00:01:14.399 --> 00:01:18.560
up to and think would be very helpful.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.810
One of the things that we also need to be
aware of is that we have some real centres

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:30.240
of excellence in almost any speciality you
care to look at in this country. In particular,

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.369
we have centres of excellence in cancer. The
Royal Marsden would be an example; Christie

00:01:34.369 --> 00:01:35.960
would be another.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:41.329
So as we enter the implementation phase, I
think we are very pleased that Cally Palmer,

00:01:41.329 --> 00:01:45.929
Chief Executive of the Royal Marsden, has
agreed to join us; because frankly, Cally,

00:01:45.929 --> 00:01:51.880
you live and breathe this stuff on a day-by-day
basis and understand the emotions, practicalities

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:57.719
and frustrations that underpin some of the
issues we have to grasp. So it was really

00:01:57.719 --> 00:02:04.259
just by way of introducing Cally to the team,
and we look forward to working with her.

00:02:04.259 --> 00:02:09.009
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: Thank you very much. If I
can just talk a little bit about progress

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:13.620
in getting the taskforce recommendations into
some kind of shape, because there are 96 of

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.570
them, we obviously need to group them and
focus on the things that are going to have

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:21.640
maximum impact on patients, while making sure
that we deliver across the patient pathway

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.150
by 2020.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:29.500
There are some very significant ambitions.
They are incredibly important, and among them

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:36.560
are saving 30,000 more lives by 2020, 11,000
through earlier diagnosis. That is an important

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.290
clue about some of the things I am going to
be saying.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:44.630
I would just like to take a few moments to
comment on some context. I would like to talk

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:47.820
about where we are doing really well, and
then comment on the areas that we really need

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:54.230
to target and improve on. There has been literally
an explosion in our knowledge and understanding

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:59.130
of better ways to diagnose and treat cancer,
globally and of course in the NHS.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:05.390
We are moving into an era where it is both
precision medicine and personalised care that

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:10.470
are incredibly important to give patients
the very best deal. We have significantly

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:17.590
improved survival in recent years, and the
NHS has placed a new and important emphasis

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:25.690
on patient experience. I talk to patients
who are going through the cancer journey regularly,

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:26.460
and have done for years.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:30.710
What is important to them is to get the best
technical care, but of course the best experience

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:35.910
of care, and certainty. That is really important
for people. I think we need to look at both

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:40.740
things. It is about embedding really high-quality
experience of care, as well as the best technical

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:47.760
treatment and outcome. Of course, the NHS
is part of the global movement, if you like,

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:53.900
to really accelerate cure and improve quality
of life for patients who live with cancer

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.280
long term.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:03.680
So, the areas that are problematic. First
of all, this is not problematic but we just

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:09.290
need to do more on prevention. Simon has already
touched on obesity and the approach to obesity,

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:15.340
which is referenced in the report. We need
to extend our work in prevention, and the

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:22.400
really critical thing is to achieve faster
access to care for patients. It is very important.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:27.430
Patients sometimes bounce around in the system.
They go through a linear diagnostic pathway,

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:31.170
with a lot of pauses in between, and we need
to correct that. We need to use resources

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:38.280
more wisely to do that. It is really important
that patients get that early definitive diagnosis,

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:43.040
because then we can start treatment earlier.
We are still seeing patients with late-stage

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:48.750
cancer where we do not need to, so we need
to get that front end of the pathway absolutely

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:49.480
right nationally.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:55.300
Of course, the other important thing to do
is to address variation, both in the delivery

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:59.720
of care - because it is different throughout
the country in terms of degree of specialism,

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:07.760
access to modern kit and facilities and specialist
workforce - but also access to care. We know

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:14.730
that there are different outcomes for people
from ethnic minorities and some older patients,

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:18.600
and we need to look at the evidence behind
that and see if we can correct it. So we must

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:24.060
address variation in delivery and access to
care.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:28.630
The taskforce report has six strategic priorities,
which I think are summarised in the report

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:34.850
for you. You will see there that they are
grouped into prevention, diagnosis, investment

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:40.460
in modern services, supporting patients living
with and beyond cancer, and really experimenting

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:45.300
with different models of commissioning and
provision, which I will come back to in a

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:45.860
moment.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:50.310
So, six strategic priorities, and we are going
to be developing work streams against all

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:58.030
those. Very importantly, the approach is going
to be that of a unified cancer programme.

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:04.260
Cancer touches many different parts of the
health system, so workforce is important,

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:09.980
kit is important and access to drugs is important.
We need to pull all that together to make

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:14.639
sure that all the impacts of the work we are
doing are in the right place.

00:06:14.639 --> 00:06:19.250
It is agreed, with the arm's-length bodies
specifically, that we will run this as a unified

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:24.060
cancer programme. You will see there is a
governance model already established, and

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:30.699
I held the first meeting of the Cancer Transformation
Board on Monday of this week. I chair that.

00:06:30.699 --> 00:06:35.630
Importantly, my good colleague Harpal is joining
me on that group. I worked with him on the

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:42.560
taskforce; he is now joining me on the transformation
board. We also have representatives from arm's-length

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:46.150
bodies and senior clinical advisers on that
board.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:53.190
That is the delivery board, if you like. I
have asked Harpal to also chair an advisory

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:58.949
group, just to make sure we have a patient
voice and the voice of charities, Royal Colleges

00:06:58.949 --> 00:07:03.290
and a wider group of stakeholders who are
able to really hold my feet to the flame,

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:09.380
I guess, in both the pace and the choice of
priorities as we roll out the taskforce recommendations

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:15.850
into the system. Harpal is organising the
first meeting of that group in early March.

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:21.449
So, a delivery system and an advisory system,
with a unified report planned to the Five-Year

00:07:21.449 --> 00:07:28.060
Forward View board by the summer, one year
on from the publication of the taskforce report.

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:37.110
That is the approach. In terms of progress
to date, I can summarise where we are. I was

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:41.620
appointed in November. I have taken a little
bit of time to get to understand what happens

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:45.120
nationally. I know London pretty well, because
I have worked at the Royal Marsden for a long

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.110
time, and I know Manchester quite well, because
of my colleagues at the Christie, which is

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:52.750
a very similar hospital to the Royal Marsden
as a specialist provider, but I have been

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:59.190
taking time to understand the pattern of national
provision and how people focus on cancer locally,

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:04.490
setting up the governance structure. We have
managed to do some good things together on

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:09.020
progressing some of those recommendations
in the last couple of months.

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:16.580
The first one of those is to test a new four-week
time-to-definitive-diagnosis standard. If

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:21.900
getting patients to a definitive diagnosis
quickly is important for their survival, their

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:28.080
experience and their outcomes, then the first
thing to do is to get a new standard working

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:32.469
that really monitors how fast people get into
that system for definitive diagnosis. We are

00:08:32.469 --> 00:08:39.860
going to be testing that in five health economies
in 2016/17, and then rolling it out fully.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:43.720
That is a new metric to really look at that
front end of the pathway that is so important.

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:49.370
The second thing is on diagnostic capacity,
and there are couple of things there. The

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:56.950
first is that we are about to trial some new
models of multidisciplinary diagnostic centres.

00:08:56.950 --> 00:09:03.020
That task to improve the diagnostic capacity
in the system and the access to specialist

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:09.670
diagnostics, I think, gets easier as the CCGs
and the GPs start federating, so we can group

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:16.860
up primary care with proper information about
cancer referral and fast access to the right

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:19.100
diagnostics for patients.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:24.270
So we are trialling some new systems. We are
looking at a national diagnostics capacity

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:29.820
fund, and Ian is working with me on national
diagnostics - beyond cancer for Ian, but cancer-specific

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:34.610
for me - to try and make sure we have the
capacity in the system, as well as fast access

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:42.980
to specialist diagnostics when required. That
is just a little bit on the four-week standard

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:45.700
and diagnostic capacity.

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:49.750
The next thing that I am planning to do, as
you will have seen from the paper, is to establish

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:57.270
a national system of cancer alliances. It
is great to have an implementation plan, but

00:09:57.270 --> 00:10:02.080
it is no good if we cannot roll it out, so
I think it is incredibly important to establish

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:06.570
a system of cancer alliances that will really
be a quality and accreditation body locally.

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:13.899
We are still working up role and function
in detail, but what this does is pull together

00:10:13.899 --> 00:10:17.740
providers and commissioners across a health
economy to look at the patient pathway together.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:27.110
So rather than a hospital seeing data on their
own performance on 62-day waits or any performance

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:32.220
gaps in terms of patient experience, primary
care and acute providers will start looking

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:37.529
together at data about patient experience
across the whole system: where the gaps occur

00:10:37.529 --> 00:10:41.600
and where the improvement plan needs to be
put in place. It is giving people more of

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:45.029
a collective responsibility for doing that.

00:10:45.029 --> 00:10:48.380
Starting with an alliance is a great thing
to do, but of course what makes an even bigger

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:54.620
difference is if you experiment with putting
the whole resource behind that and commissioning

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:56.029
by care pathways.

00:10:56.029 --> 00:11:00.990
So we are looking at a national system of
cancer alliances, with a trajectory towards

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:06.750
an experiment under the new care model system,
where we are looking at accountable cancer

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:10.490
networks. It is drawing on some of the experience
in the States, trying to make sure it is fit

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:16.670
for purpose for the National Health Service,
and it is looking at care-based commissioning

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:19.160
from April 2017.

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:23.200
So it is an experiment. The alliances are
not an experiment. They are a good thing to

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:30.260
do, to give cancer the focus. The full accountable
cancer network is a really exciting pilot.

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:35.160
We are currently piloting that at the Royal
Marsden and partners, at the Christie and

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:38.830
partners, and at University College London
and partners.

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:45.190
Together, those cover a population of 10 million,
so it is a decent-sized pilot. The alliances

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:49.149
and the accountable cancer networks are about
the local levers for really creating a different

00:11:49.149 --> 00:11:54.649
view about speed into the system, and then
managing the pathway as a group of providers

00:11:54.649 --> 00:11:58.130
rather than looking at individual sections
of it.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:02.680
To support the alliances and the accountable
cancer networks, you will have seen a couple

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:09.010
of slightly blurry screenshots. Sorry they
are not in high definition in the papers,

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:13.700
but the idea here is that we have started
a model of an integrated cancer dashboard.

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:15.660
There are two things.

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:20.100
Firstly, this word I keep using: an integrated
set of metrics for the providers across a

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:26.120
health economy to use. The second really important
thing about that integrated cancer dashboard

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:31.820
is that it has new metrics about speed into
the system and quality of life, as well as

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:34.709
more sophisticated measures about patient
experience.

00:12:34.709 --> 00:12:43.220
You will see that the suggestion is that we
start being able to assess how many patients

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:49.149
come into the system at stage one or stage
two of their disease. Cancer is usually staged

00:12:49.149 --> 00:12:54.500
up to stage four; we need to see early-stage
cancers presenting to the system more broadly

00:12:54.500 --> 00:13:00.089
and widely, and to see that improve and increase.
We also need to start thinking about quality

00:13:00.089 --> 00:13:05.230
of life for those who need to be supported
with cancer, beyond their journey through

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:08.800
the hospital system.

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:13.600
Also on the dashboard, quite importantly,
is a metric about percentage of patients who

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:21.160
present through emergency admission, because
we know that patients who come in as an emergency

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:26.240
tend to do less well. We need to reduce that
emergency presentation and improve the planning,

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:28.180
as well as the speed into the system.

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:34.930
So that is really the progress so far. I have
agreed with Harpal that we will provide a

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:39.779
unified report from the advisory group and
the transformation board to the Five-Year

00:13:39.779 --> 00:13:44.769
Forward View board in the summer, one year
on, and I look forward to talking again to

00:13:44.769 --> 00:13:49.399
this Board about the progress we are making,
but I will pause there. Happy to take any

00:13:49.399 --> 00:13:49.680
questions.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:54.279
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Cally.
I know the Board will wish to track progress

00:13:54.279 --> 00:13:59.730
very closely on this as one of our major six
clinical priority areas. Let me invite questions

00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:02.959
and comments from the Board. Noel.

00:14:02.959 --> 00:14:10.180
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: It is a terrific programme
of work that you have in mind, Cally. I am

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:17.459
just wondering, from a patient perspective
or indeed a patient experience perspective,

00:14:17.459 --> 00:14:26.170
what are going to be the early wins? What
will you deliver to patients in the next 12

00:14:26.170 --> 00:14:32.120
months? Do you envisage that coming as sort
of waterfalls of improvement, or progressive

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:34.010
increments of improvement?

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:44.990
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: Both very good questions.
For patients, I think the very early work

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:49.010
around improving what we collect in patient
experience - so talking to patients about

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:53.230
their experience of care - I think we can
do more in terms of both collecting their

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:56.350
views and information and responding to them.

00:14:56.350 --> 00:15:00.140
I think creating and setting up the national
cancer alliance structure is really important,

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:04.380
because that is about focusing on your local
community and working out what matters to

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:08.300
them. I can sit at the centre and say it is
these three or four things, but actually we

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:12.459
need to be able to respond to local circumstances
with a focused alliance vehicle that has the

00:15:12.459 --> 00:15:18.380
metrics behind it to spot and support where
there are gaps in the system. While it is

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:22.790
still a structural thing, I think creating
that focus on cancer and looking at patient

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:27.720
experience for local communities will be an
important early thing to do.

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:34.050
We will also be introducing new diagnostic
models in 2016/17, so it is the start of a

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:38.800
programme to try and make sure that more patients
get into the system faster. With that, we

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:43.810
need to improve information to GPs and patients
about choice and access.

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:47.680
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: So you would expect RTT to
share an uptick?

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:54.339
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: Yes. It is creating an improvement
plan where there are gaps as fast as we can,

00:15:54.339 --> 00:15:58.639
by getting people to work differently and
understanding where the issues are for patients

00:15:58.639 --> 00:16:02.199
better. We can make significant progress on
that during 2016/17.

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:12.940
In terms of whether it is a micro or macro
journey, I think it depends what the issue

00:16:12.940 --> 00:16:21.720
is. If you are talking about modernising equipment,
that is quite a big step. If it is about rolling

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:26.220
out new diagnostic models or the alliance
model, then I think it is an incremental step.

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:31.180
However, I will be absolutely clear that we
need to have some evaluation metric, so this

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:33.950
is not just moving forward an inch.

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:39.180
This is really clearly saying this is the
plan for 2016/17 - impact on patients, impact

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:43.529
on efficiency - and then we will measure how
we are doing. There is no point rolling out

00:16:43.529 --> 00:16:48.089
loads of new measures and then not being able
to evaluate them properly, so that will go

00:16:48.089 --> 00:16:49.200
along with it. Hopefully that gives you a
bit of reassurance.

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:58.000
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Can I ask a question about
variation? My understanding is 
it is quite marked, particularly around poverty

00:16:58.000 --> 00:16:58.450
and race.

00:16:58.450 --> 00:16:59.360
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: It is.

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:05.130
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: I wonder if, in a sense,
that is low-hanging fruit, is it not? I wonder

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:11.289
what your thoughts are on where you start,
because obviously if you start with everyone,

00:17:11.289 --> 00:17:14.530
the people who have always benefited will
benefit, if you see what I mean. This is a

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:19.419
classic case of the inverse care law, so I
just wonder what your thoughts are on where

00:17:19.419 --> 00:17:20.150
you start?

00:17:20.150 --> 00:17:26.709
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: Again, a very good question.
I think it is quite important, as we start

00:17:26.709 --> 00:17:33.780
to look by community and by care pathway,
to target the areas of greatest need. As I

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:39.480
say, there are 96 recommendations but I am
all for simplicity and focus. We know that

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:46.580
survival rates differ by 10% or more per CCG.
We know that black and ethnic minority patients

00:17:46.580 --> 00:17:50.970
do not get the same access, so there has been
some research done on that. We know the things

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:56.110
that we are trying to fix. We need to get
this local focus right, and the local improvement

00:17:56.110 --> 00:18:00.210
plan right for that particular population.
It will be different in different parts of

00:18:00.210 --> 00:18:02.030
the country or different areas.

00:18:02.030 --> 00:18:10.280
It is partly a sort of information and brief,
partly a change in pathway and partly a very

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:13.450
clear plan with some evaluation against it
so that we know we are tackling the areas

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:18.710
which will have the most impact. This strategy
is about big impact. It is not about tweaking

00:18:18.710 --> 00:18:21.120
the edges for where care is good.

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:24.789
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Just one follow-up question.
I know we have the mental health taskforce

00:18:24.789 --> 00:18:31.169
- it was mentioned - but cancer and mental
health are closely related. I just wonder

00:18:31.169 --> 00:18:37.080
about further support, or what thoughts you
have about how we strengthen the link between

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:41.010
physical and emotional support for people
with cancer?

00:18:41.010 --> 00:18:47.340
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: There are a couple of answers
to that. The first is that one of the particular

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:52.890
requirements in the report is to think about
psychological support for patients going through

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:58.490
cancer. There is quite a lot of evidence about
the support people need, and we know it is

00:18:58.490 --> 00:18:59.000
patchy.

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:03.190
Within the cancer programme itself, we need
to think about the support available to patients

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:07.669
who have either a mental health issue before
they come in or psychological issues with

00:19:07.669 --> 00:19:12.220
their treatment - or post treatment, with
late effects. That is very clearly a requirement

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:13.740
in the report.

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:17.330
The second thing, which I have not done yet
- it is early days - but need to do is to

00:19:17.330 --> 00:19:21.340
talk in more detail to my colleagues who are
leading these other programmes of work - particularly

00:19:21.340 --> 00:19:25.280
mental health - so that we can connect up
in an appropriate way. I am not there yet,

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:28.790
but I know that is something I need to do.

00:19:28.790 --> 00:19:34.500
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Just to touch on another
aspect of variation, which is variation in

00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:38.470
clinical treatment and, of course, what we
are trying to do on the Right Care initiative.

00:19:38.470 --> 00:19:44.790
We know that, globally, there is quite significant
variation - in particular, the point at which

00:19:44.790 --> 00:19:49.720
therapy which must change to palliation - and
I wondered whether this was part of the work

00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:52.760
that you are doing with the taskforce implementation?

00:19:52.760 --> 00:20:00.570
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: It is. There are different
approaches globally to both symptom control

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:08.740
and end of life care. Both for the report
and for me personally, a priority is to look

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:13.820
at every aspect of patient treatment. While
I have laboured the point about getting patients

00:20:13.820 --> 00:20:20.900
in fast so that we can improve survival rates,
there are two things that are really important

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:25.049
to me about symptom control, palliation and
end-of-life care, which are reflected in the

00:20:25.049 --> 00:20:26.140
report.

00:20:26.140 --> 00:20:30.610
The first is that symptom control and palliation
need to be planned early, not at the end of

00:20:30.610 --> 00:20:34.370
acute treatment. You need to start that early,
and I am talking to clinicians about how we

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:39.610
do that better. It is a cultural thing in
terms of treatment planning. It is better

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:44.260
for a patient if both the acute phase and
also the palliation and symptom control are

00:20:44.260 --> 00:20:46.880
planned at the beginning.

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:52.880
In terms of end-of-life care, there is some
very good work going on in the country. The

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:57.080
key thing is to give patients choice about
where they want to die, because too many people

00:20:57.080 --> 00:21:01.900
die in hospital. I was taught very early on
at the Royal Marsden that there is such a

00:21:01.900 --> 00:21:06.870
thing as a good death. You have to look at
choice for people and reverse the dynamic

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:08.820
about where people want to die.

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:13.400
More people tend to want to die in their homes,
or in a hospice locally, than in hospitals.

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:20.580
We need to change that dynamic by connecting
up care records and giving GPs, ambulance

00:21:20.580 --> 00:21:28.390
crews, families and others support to allow
patients to be cared for at home. It is very

00:21:28.390 --> 00:21:32.480
important that we look at the whole thing,
not just acute interventions to achieve survival.

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:41.090
&gt;&gt;John Burn: As you know, I have a special
interest in colorectal cancer, both diagnostically

00:21:41.090 --> 00:21:46.390
and research-wise. Just to flag up one of
the issues that has been picked up, I am delighted

00:21:46.390 --> 00:21:51.470
to see the expansion in non-medical endoscopists
listed in the early priorities.

00:21:51.470 --> 00:21:55.250
Also, the Secretary of State's commitment
to vastly expanding the amount of genetic

00:21:55.250 --> 00:22:00.160
testing we do on tumours, as you know, is
inevitably going to lead to a lot more identification

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:05.169
of people who need colonoscopies, and that
needs to be medical colonoscopies, not non-medical

00:22:05.169 --> 00:22:06.270
endoscopists.

00:22:06.270 --> 00:22:14.260
So my question is: at what point, if not already,
are we involving the training programme for

00:22:14.260 --> 00:22:18.510
medical endoscopists and making sure that
we are expanding that workforce, which, we

00:22:18.510 --> 00:22:22.480
are already seeing, is becoming a critical
barrier to managing these families?

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:28.480
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: A very important question.
It is the reason why the Cancer Transformation

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:33.320
Board has senior representation from Health
Education England, and I have already talked

00:22:33.320 --> 00:22:42.140
to my colleagues there about the issues around
workforce. I think we can do quite a lot by

00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:47.100
changing models and working more efficiently,
but inevitably there will be some lead-in

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:52.049
times in terms of development and training
of workforce. It is very, very important to

00:22:52.049 --> 00:22:56.809
work alongside Health Education England in
ensuring that we tackle those issues to create

00:22:56.809 --> 00:22:57.730
that capacity.

00:22:57.730 --> 00:23:01.570
There is no point thinking about it in three
years' time; we need to do it now, which is

00:23:01.570 --> 00:23:06.630
the point you are making. So that is all under
control, in the sense that they are represented

00:23:06.630 --> 00:23:11.320
and working with me on the Transformation
Board, and it is a key part of the taskforce

00:23:11.320 --> 00:23:14.760
report, as they know.

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:23.539
&gt;&gt;Margaret Casely-Hayford: A splendid report.
Really pleased to see the push, through the

00:23:23.539 --> 00:23:30.460
national alliance, to improve implementation
of the strategy and getting the right metrics

00:23:30.460 --> 00:23:37.789
in place so that they are owned right across
the piece. That is really excellent. All of

00:23:37.789 --> 00:23:43.970
this will, as you say, help to reduce emergency
presentation, but there will inevitably be

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:47.250
emergency presentation.

00:23:47.250 --> 00:24:03.470
My question is really linked to Victor's.
When people are at that stage of acute distress,

00:24:03.470 --> 00:24:11.320
there is a need for specialist awareness at
that point. I am not seeing anything here

00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:19.030
that is really pointing to metrics that are
measuring how we improve that front-end response

00:24:19.030 --> 00:24:30.159
so that there is a better patient experience.
I am very keen that, at that stage, we can

00:24:30.159 --> 00:24:36.480
actually not just identify but measure the
improvements.

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:42.789
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: That is, again, a very important
point. There are a couple of responses. The

00:24:42.789 --> 00:24:48.070
first is that clearly we are trying to measure
whether we can get that number down. You are

00:24:48.070 --> 00:24:52.809
right; you will always find patients who will
present through A&amp;E, so we need to take care

00:24:52.809 --> 00:24:57.270
of that. The first thing to do is to make
sure we know that we can reduce that figure,

00:24:57.270 --> 00:25:00.220
and we measure how we are doing country-wide
on that.

00:25:00.220 --> 00:25:04.450
The second thing, which we have introduced
but need to roll out further, is something

00:25:04.450 --> 00:25:09.669
called an acute oncology service, where you
have a connected consultant system between

00:25:09.669 --> 00:25:17.640
a specialist centre - it is really an outreach
- working into A&amp;E departments and other cancer

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:22.429
units to pick up patients through A&amp;E, and
make sure early advice is given. We need to

00:25:22.429 --> 00:25:27.150
just model that and roll that out more broadly
because, where it is in operation, it really

00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:31.950
speeds up the pathway and improves the experience
for patients. We just need more of that. It

00:25:31.950 --> 00:25:35.460
is an important part of the improvement journey,
if I can put it that way.

00:25:35.460 --> 00:25:36.200
&gt;&gt;Margaret Casely-Hayford: That is really
helpful. Thank you.

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:40.650
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: To echo my colleagues: great
stuff. A really good, clear programme, so

00:25:40.650 --> 00:25:50.110
thank you for that. A couple of questions.
The first is that I know from my own experience

00:25:50.110 --> 00:25:57.260
the really central role that the voluntary
sector plays in supporting people through

00:25:57.260 --> 00:26:02.450
various different aspects of a journey through
cancer. I would be interested if you could

00:26:02.450 --> 00:26:07.440
speak a bit about the extent to which you
are engaging and involving the voluntary sector

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:13.159
and trying to stimulate that sector to support
in a co ordinated and consistent way.

00:26:13.159 --> 00:26:20.549
The second question really is: all the things
you are raise are essential, in terms of getting

00:26:20.549 --> 00:26:26.510
done, and other than raiding Mr Baumann's
piggy bank for more money, I would be interested

00:26:26.510 --> 00:26:31.770
to know what the one or two things are that
this Board could do to really increase your

00:26:31.770 --> 00:26:35.840
chances of success. What is the help that
we could provide you with, and an area where

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:42.980
we might be able to help free some barriers?
So voluntary sector and then, where can we

00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:43.880
help you?

00:26:43.880 --> 00:26:49.350
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: Okay, thank you. So, very
importantly the advisory group I have mentioned

00:26:49.350 --> 00:26:54.070
will have a wide representation, because it
is very important that we do not lose the

00:26:54.070 --> 00:26:58.860
value of all the people who get involved in
trying to improve cancer services in this

00:26:58.860 --> 00:26:59.350
country.

00:26:59.350 --> 00:27:05.169
I think the voluntary sector and charities
have really soaked into a lot of space to

00:27:05.169 --> 00:27:10.870
try and improve patients' experience living
with and beyond cancer, and early diagnostics.

00:27:10.870 --> 00:27:17.059
So what I am trying to do is to take the lead
through the Transformation Board but also

00:27:17.059 --> 00:27:21.090
to involve those people, but probably principally
through the work of the advisory group that

00:27:21.090 --> 00:27:25.159
Harpal leads. They will hold up a mirror to
the work that we are doing with the arm's

00:27:25.159 --> 00:27:31.490
length bodies and senior clinicians on the
Transformation Board, but it is terribly important

00:27:31.490 --> 00:27:33.470
that it is connected up in this unified way.

00:27:33.470 --> 00:27:39.450
So I have not forgotten them, and it is very
important that they continue to advise. They

00:27:39.450 --> 00:27:43.029
advised on the taskforce recommendations and
they will advise on the implementation plan,

00:27:43.029 --> 00:27:47.610
so we can make sure that connection exists.
So I feel strongly about that, and I agree

00:27:47.610 --> 00:27:49.909
it is important.

00:27:49.909 --> 00:28:02.850
In terms of where this Board can help, I think
the first is holding me to account, so it

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:07.929
is asking me questions, like the ones we have
just had, on, 'Okay, so what is the impact

00:28:07.929 --> 00:28:09.620
on patients in 2016 and 2017?'

00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:15.059
It is very easy for me to get very involved
in diagnostic models, alliance models, role

00:28:15.059 --> 00:28:21.850
and function; sometimes you just need to think
about the headlines, and so there is just

00:28:21.850 --> 00:28:26.140
a simple thing about asking me what we are
doing for patients each time I come and talk

00:28:26.140 --> 00:28:31.299
to you. It just helps me focus on exactly
the right things, if that does not sound too

00:28:31.299 --> 00:28:31.770
trite.

00:28:31.770 --> 00:28:36.870
I think the second thing is, this programme
is going to require capital and revenue. I

00:28:36.870 --> 00:28:46.059
will say two things about that. The first
is that I have worked for the Health Service

00:28:46.059 --> 00:28:50.289
for 35 years; I am used to working in a cash
limited system and doing the best I can with

00:28:50.289 --> 00:28:54.010
available resource, and I expect to do that
with this programme, just like I would with

00:28:54.010 --> 00:28:55.659
the delivery of care at the Royal Marsden.

00:28:55.659 --> 00:29:00.650
So it is natural territory to look at what
you have and then do the absolute best job

00:29:00.650 --> 00:29:05.470
you can, so the key is prioritising how you
use that resource for most patient benefit.

00:29:05.470 --> 00:29:09.390
That is normal life, really, if you work in
the Health Service.

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:15.490
So it is going to require some investment,
but my job is to use whatever resource is

00:29:15.490 --> 00:29:19.809
available to best effect and I am entirely
happy, and expect, to do that.

00:29:19.809 --> 00:29:27.279
Sorry, I have forgotten the second thing I
was going to say about money.

00:29:27.279 --> 00:29:35.429
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Cally, you should not regard
the opportunity as being one off, there will

00:29:35.429 --> 00:29:38.750
be further opportunities to engage with us
and bring further requests.

00:29:38.750 --> 00:29:45.450
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: I know what it was: the point
I made to Simon when I was first appointed.

00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:49.370
Because I have worked in the system with cancer
for so long, I can see where you can save

00:29:49.370 --> 00:29:56.460
so much. You need to kick start some investment
but I can see, by targeting therapy, by stopping

00:29:56.460 --> 00:30:02.990
inappropriate diagnostic testing, where you
can save money; so rolling out molecular diagnostics,

00:30:02.990 --> 00:30:08.340
improving targeting of therapy, changing the
pathway for patients so it is shorter and

00:30:08.340 --> 00:30:08.590
better.

00:30:08.590 --> 00:30:13.230
I can actually see, with a bit of kick start
investment, how we can improve the resource.

00:30:13.230 --> 00:30:19.549
The ultimate job is to make sure we can spend
the most resource, both preventing and then

00:30:19.549 --> 00:30:23.210
dealing with early diagnosis and treatment,
as well as, of course, supporting people who

00:30:23.210 --> 00:30:28.370
do have advanced cancer. It is about shifting
that resource so that we can really improve

00:30:28.370 --> 00:30:30.480
survival at an early stage.

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:34.950
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I think two observations
there, one of which is the way in which we

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:39.850
can link that understanding into the right
care programme to try and use that as a vehicle

00:30:39.850 --> 00:30:47.909
to spread practice that will improve outcomes
and efficiency. The second is that I know

00:30:47.909 --> 00:30:52.000
that you are programmed into our plans for
the commissioning committee, and I think we

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:57.559
would love to hear not just progress on what
you are doing but also your thoughts, because

00:30:57.559 --> 00:31:02.320
to get a serving Chief Executive is really
helpful in terms of saying here are three,

00:31:02.320 --> 00:31:05.320
four, five things that, if you focus on this,
actually there are outcome benefits but there

00:31:05.320 --> 00:31:10.159
are also efficiency benefits. How we can spread
that would be really interesting and useful,

00:31:10.159 --> 00:31:11.190
so thank you.

00:31:11.190 --> 00:31:12.549
&gt;&gt;Cally Palmer: Thank you.

00:31:12.549 --> 00:31:17.159
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Right, well, Cally, thank
you very much. I think we are very fortunate

00:31:17.159 --> 00:31:22.470
to have you on board. It has been quite a
short period since you have been with us and

00:31:22.470 --> 00:31:28.429
yet we see, I think, a very clearly sketched
out implementation plan. So I am sure we will

00:31:28.429 --> 00:31:34.080
meet one of your requests to us, which is
to hold you to account, but I think everything

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:34.760
else that you have said to us this morning
fills us with confidence that this is going

00:31:34.760 --> 00:31:40.009
in the right direction, and we look forward
to supporting you and working with you.

